INT. MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - VAULT CORRIDOR - NIGHT

Marble, silence, a thousand brass hearts ticking behind glass. A single laser, thin as a pencil line, hums across the corridor at ankle height.

VESPER, 30s, in black from throat to boot, crouches before a keypad with a gloved thumb on her lip. ANTON KROL, 50s, her partner, broad and patient, kneels beside her holding a stethoscope against the vault door as if listening to a sleeping animal.

VESPER
Seven. Four. One.

ANTON KROL
(simultaneously)
Seven. Four. One.

^ANTON KROL
Eight.

VESPER
Eight?

ANTON KROL
The clock in the second room. It runs eight minutes fast.

The keypad flickers green. Then red. The ticking in the walls goes still, all at once.

VESPER
(softly)
Anton. Every clock just stopped.

Anton lifts the stethoscope from the door and holds out his open hand, palm flat, as if testing for rain.

ANTON KROL
Then somebody else is in here.

Vesper slides her pistol out. The laser along the floor blinks off.
